{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>And every terumah of all the holy things of the children of Israel</b> — He says that the terumah which the children of Israel designate, that is to say that which they set aside and consecrate, shall belong to the priest. For since terumah has no fixed measure, Scripture says that whatever they separate from it shall belong to the priest, when the owners present it to give it to him — not that the priest may take it with a show of force. And this is the simple meaning of the verse.\n\nThus they said in Sifrei (Naso 30): Rabbi Akiva says, Scripture comes to teach you that if he wished to make his entire threshing floor terumah, he may do so, provided he leaves over a small portion.\n\nAnd they further taught there in Sifrei: \"אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיבוּ לַכֹּהֵן\" (\"which they present to the priest\") — Rabbi Yishmael said: But is terumah presented to the priest? What does the verse teach by saying \"אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיבוּ לַכֹּהֵן לוֹ יִהְיֶה\" (\"which they present to the priest, to him it shall belong\")? Because it says (Exodus 23:19) \"רֵאשִׁית בִּכּוּרֵי אַדְמָתְךָ\" (\"the first of the first fruits of your land\") etc., but we have not heard what is done with them — Scripture teaches by saying \"אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיבוּ לַכֹּהֵן לוֹ יִהְיֶה\" — Scripture comes and teaches concerning the first fruits that they are given to the priest.\n\nAnd Rashi explained (Rashi on Numbers 5:9): But is terumah presented to the priest? Does he not himself go around collecting it in the granaries? And this is not clear.\n\nRather, its explanation follows the opinion of Rabbi Yishmael: the language of הקרבה (presentation/offering) does not occur in the Torah except for things offered upon the altar; therefore he derives \"אֲשֶׁר יַקְרִיבוּ\" with reference to the first fruits, which require waving and presentation. Scripture says that all the first fruits which the owners present to HaShem — to the priest who presents them, it shall belong. And what he said, that since it is stated \"תָּבִיא בֵּית ה' אֱלֹהֶיךָ\" (\"you shall bring to the house of YHWH your Elohim\") and I do not know what is done with them — this is to say that we have still not learned concerning the first fruits that they are among the gifts of priesthood at all, and here Scripture first teaches concerning them that they are given to the priest.\n\nBut afterward He returned and repeated it in the section dealing with priesthood (Numbers 18:13): \"בִּכּוּרֵי כָּל אֲשֶׁר בְּאַרְצָם לְךָ יִהְיֶה\" (\"the first fruits of all that is in their land, to you they shall belong\"), for there He repeated also terumah and holy gifts, in order to include all the gifts of priesthood together and to establish with all of them a covenant of salt (ibid. v. 19), and further for matters that were newly stated there in that section, \"כָּל טָהוֹר בְּבֵיתְךָ יֹאכְלֶנּוּ\" (ibid. v. 13). And we further learn from here that the first fruits belong to the men of the watch who present them, and they do not belong to any priest who wishes, unlike terumah."], :book :numbers, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 5, :notes {0 "In the closing line, Ramban contrasts first fruits (given to the priest of the current watch serving in the Temple) with terumah (which may be given to any priest of one's choosing). The phrase אנשי משמר refers to the priestly division (mishmar) on duty."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:03:25.754265075Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}